.TH BAHAI-DATE 1 "October 2026" "bahai-date 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bahai-date \- convert between Gregorian and Baha'i (Badi) calendar dates
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bahai-date
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ COMMAND ]
.PP
Available commands:
.BR today " (the default when no command is given),"
.BR to\-badi ,
.BR to\-greg ,
.BR next\-holy\-day ,
.BR holy\-days .
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bahai-date
converts between Gregorian and Badi (Baha'i) calendar dates, watches the
Baha'i year progress, and counts down to feasts and holy days. It works
entirely offline.
.PP
The Baha'i day begins at
.B sunset,
not midnight, so dates roll over in the evening. Sunset is computed for the
coordinates given with
.B \-\-lat
and
.B \-\-lon
(default: Bahji, 32.9434 N, 35.0924 E) in the timezone given with
.B \-\-timezone
(default: system timezone, falling back to Asia/Jerusalem).
.PP
Date output always includes the month, weekday, and Vahid-year names with
their English meanings. The original Arabic and Persian scripts can be added
with
.BR \-\-arabic ,
.BR \-\-persian ,
or
.B \-\-arfa
(see
.BR "NAME SCRIPTS" ).
.PP
With no subcommand,
.B today
is assumed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Today's date:
.nf
    $ bahai-date
    9 Mashíyyat (Will) 183
    Weekday: Kamál (Perfection)
    Year 183 Javáb (Answer) · Vahid 10 · Kull-i-Shay 1
    Gregorian: 2026-10-04 17:29 EDT
    Timezone: America/New_York
.fi
.TP
Convert a Gregorian date to Baha'i (the time of day matters, since days
start at sunset \- 18:30 after Naw\-Ruz sunset is already the 2nd of the year):
.nf
    $ bahai-date to\-badi \-Y 2024 \-M 3 \-D 20 \-H 18 \-I 30 \-t Asia/Jerusalem
    2 Bahá (Splendor) 181
    Weekday: Istijlál (Majesty)
    Year 181 Hubb (Love) · Vahid 10 · Kull-i-Shay 1
    Gregorian: 2024-03-20 18:30 IST
    Timezone: Asia/Jerusalem
.fi
.TP
Convert a Baha'i date to Gregorian:
.nf
    $ bahai-date to\-greg \-Y 181 \-M 1 \-D 1 \-t Asia/Jerusalem
    1 Bahá (Splendor) 181
    Weekday: 'Idál (Justice)
    Year 181 Hubb (Love) · Vahid 10 · Kull-i-Shay 1
    Gregorian: 2024-03-19 17:50 IST (starts at sunset)
    Timezone: Asia/Jerusalem
    Naw-Rúz (work suspended)
    Nineteen Day Feast of Bahá
.fi
.TP
One line for widgets and scripts:
.nf
    $ bahai-date \-\-text
    9 Mashíyyat (Will) 183
.fi
.TP
Days until the next Nineteen Day Feast:
.nf
    $ bahai-date \-\-countdown feast
    11 days
.fi
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
All flags are global: they work with any subcommand, before or after it.
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-timezone \fITIMEZONE\fR
IANA timezone, e.g. America/New_York (spaces are OK: America/New York).
Default: system timezone, falling back to Asia/Jerusalem.
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-lat \fILAT\fR
Latitude for sunset calculation. Default: 32.9434 (Bahji).
.TP
.B \-L, \-\-lon \fILON\fR
Longitude for sunset calculation. Default: 35.0924 (Bahji).
.TP
.B \-\-arabic
Include Arabic-script names of months, weekdays, and Vahid years.
.TP
.B \-\-persian
Include Persian-script names of months, weekdays, and Vahid years.
.TP
.B \-\-arfa
Include both Arabic- and Persian-script names. Identical spellings are
shown once.
.TP
.B \-\-text
Print just the date as a single plain line \- no line breaks, no box, no
emoji, no color. Intended for widgets, Emacs, and other command consumers.
.TP
.B \-\-text\-all
Like
.BR \-\-text ,
but includes weekday, Vahid year, cycle position, and any annotations.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-text .
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-fancy
Emoji and color output (uses
.BR gum (1)
when available) for today, to\-badi, to\-greg, next\-holy\-day, and holy\-days.
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-progress \fR[\fIENTRY\fR]
With an ENTRY (day, month, year, vahid, epoch; aliases kull-i-shay,
kullishay), print a single ASCII progress bar for that span. Bare, or with
.BR all ,
clear the screen and show a full dashboard of all five spans plus the next
holy day, then wait for Enter.
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-countdown \fR[\fIBUCKET\fR]
With a BUCKET (day, feast or month, holy-day or holyday, year, nawruz or
naw\-ruz, vahid; alias
.BR \-\-cd ),
print one plain line with the time remaining. Bare, or with
.BR all ,
print an annotated summary of all buckets. Days until Ayyam-i-Ha, years
until the next Vahid, and Vahids until the next Kull-i-Shay are included.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print help.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print version.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B today
Today's Baha'i date (the default when no subcommand is given).
.TP
.B to\-badi
Convert a Gregorian date to Baha'i.
Options:
.B \-Y, \-\-year
(Gregorian year),
.B \-M, \-\-month
(1\-12),
.B \-D, \-\-day
(1\-31),
.B \-H, \-\-hour
(0\-23, default 12),
.B \-I, \-\-minute
(0\-59, default 0).
.TP
.B to\-greg
Convert a Baha'i date to Gregorian.
Options:
.B \-Y, \-\-year
(Baha'i year, e.g. 181),
.B \-M, \-\-month
(0 = Ayyam-i-Ha, 1\-19),
.B \-D, \-\-day
(1\-19).
.TP
.B next\-holy\-day
Next upcoming holy day: name, Badi and Gregorian dates, days away, and
celebration time.
.TP
.B holy\-days
List all holy days for the current Baha'i year, chronologically, with work
suspension status.
.SH OUTPUT MODES
.TP
.B Plain (default)
Line-oriented and stable for scripts: the Baha'i date, the weekday name, the
year with its position in the Vahid and Kull-i-Shay cycles, the Gregorian
date/time, and the timezone, followed by any annotations (holy day,
Ayyam-i-Ha day, Feast).
.TP
.B \-\-text / \-\-text\-all
A single line with parts joined by
.RB ` \(m7 ',
for widgets and scripts.
.B \-\-text
prints only the date;
.B \-\-text\-all
adds weekday, Vahid year, cycles, and annotations. Both override
.BR \-\-fancy .
.TP
.B \-\-fancy
Rounded box via
.BR gum (1)
with month emoji and highlighted holy days, Ayyam-i-Ha, and Feast days.
.TP
Precedence:
.B \-\-progress
wins over
.BR \-\-countdown ,
which wins over
.B \-\-text / \-\-text\-all ,
which win over
.BR \-\-fancy .
.SH NAME SCRIPTS
Month, weekday, and Vahid-year names always print with their English
meanings.
.B \-\-arabic
and
.B \-\-persian
add the respective scripts;
.B \-\-arfa
adds both. The
.B holy\-days
table keeps fixed-width columns and always uses plain month names.
.SH THE BADI CALENDAR
The Badi calendar has 19 months of 19 days each, the intercalary days of
Ayyam-i-Ha (4\-5 days between months 18 and 19), and years grouped into
19\-year Vahid cycles; 19 Vahids make up a Kull-i-Shay (361 years). Naw-Ruz
(the new year) falls on March 20\-21. The Badi day starts at sunset, so each
weekday begins the evening before its Gregorian counterpart (Jalal begins at
Friday sunset).
